WEBVTT

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.000
Aminjon Niyazov was born
in the Fergana Valley

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.600
and came of age
in a time of upheaval.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.560
From helping the hungry as a young man
to work in construction,

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:15.720
finance, and government, his public
service spanned decades of change.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.240
He went on to lead Soviet Uzbekistan

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:21.520
and continued to work
after leaving office.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:27.280
This is the story of his life, his family,
and the country he served.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:47.310
He was born in the autumn
of nineteen-oh-three in Ak-Tepe,

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:49.369
a village outside Margilan,

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:54.810
in the Fergana Valley; on a party form he
filled in himself in nineteen twenty-four,

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:57.310
he gave the date as the first of October.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:01.950
Margilan was a city of silk weavers,
famous for its ikats.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:04.610
A generation before he was born,

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.550
the valley had been the heart
of the Khanate of Kokand,

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:12.710
until Russia annexed it, in eighteen
seventy-six, as the province of Fergana.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:16.749
On that same form he wrote
that his grandfather and father

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:20.429
were peasant farmers,
working a plot of a little over a hectare,

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.270
and that his mother kept the house.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:27.410
He had two years at the Russian-native
school in Margilan, and did not finish.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:30.410
Then the empire fell.

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:34.829
Revolution and civil war cut
Turkestan off from Russian grain,

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:38.050
and famine may have killed
as many as a million people;

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.990
the hungry lived on grass and oil cake.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.262
In nineteen nineteen, at fifteen,

00:01:44.263 --> 00:01:48.170
Aminjon Niyazov went to work
for the Fergana district food committee.

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:50.869
He handed out food as one of its stewards,

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:53.670
and chaired its commission
for aid to the starving.

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:57.950
That year he joined the Komsomol,
the Communist youth league,

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:01.270
and he went on to lead its committee
in the town of Fergana.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:06.809
From nineteen twenty to nineteen
twenty-two he served in the town

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:09.549
of Fergana as an assistant
chief of the militia,

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:13.410
and as an officer of the Cheka,
the Bolshevik political police.

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:18.610
These were the years when the Basmachi
insurgency, as the Soviets called it,

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:23.950
held much of the valley, and the militia's
first task was to fight its armed bands.

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:27.510
His biography recalls what
those bands could mean.

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:31.639
His future wife, Salimakhon,
was orphaned at ten,

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:35.540
when the band of the Basmachi leader
Kurshirmat killed her parents,

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:37.980
farmers who had no food to give it.

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:43.260
In nineteen twenty-four,
Moscow redrew the map of Central Asia,

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.140
and the Fergana Valley became part
of a new Uzbek Soviet Socialist Republic.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:53.359
In nineteen twenty-five Aminjon Niyazov,
by now a Communist,

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.640
became secretary of the provincial
commission for the land and water reform,

00:02:58.120 --> 00:02:59.879
which took land and water rights

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.080
from wealthy landowners and gave
them to landless farmers.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.420
Some refused the land.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:10.440
I will die of hunger, they said,
but I will not take another man's land.

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:12.780
For his work on the reform,

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:18.092
the republic's government awarded him an
honorary diploma in nineteen twenty-six.

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:31.429
In May nineteen twenty-six he
took over the finance department

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:36.129
of the Fergana region, and in April
nineteen twenty-seven he became deputy

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:40.810
head of construction of the new textile
mill at Fergana, named after Dzerzhinsky;

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:45.250
when its chief was away, the orders
went out over Niyazov's signature.

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:49.949
Some nine hundred young men and women
were sent to Russia's textile centres

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:53.930
to learn the trade, and came home
to work the new mills of Central Asia.

00:03:55.150 --> 00:04:00.050
In nineteen thirty, the First Secretary
of the Uzbek party, Akmal Ikramov,

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:03.890
offered him a place in a special
group being sent to study in Germany.

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:07.950
With a young wife, Salimakhon,
and their first child,

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:12.850
Niyazov asked instead to study in Moscow,
where his family could join him,

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:16.890
and Ikramov recommended him
to the All-Union Industrial Academy,

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:20.550
a school that turned party
men into industrial managers.

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:23.829
The Academy's party secretary was

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:27.690
a former metal fitter from the Donbas
named Nikita Khrushchev.

00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:30.490
Niyazov later recalled
one of his meetings.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:34.349
Khrushchev argued from the tribune
that future engineers

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:38.490
had no need of the social sciences,
and carried the vote after midnight.

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:41.429
Next morning a letter from Stalin arrived,

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.790
and the meeting reversed
itself unanimously.

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:47.869
Within months Khrushchev left
for party work in the capital.

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:51.090
Their paths would cross again,
in Tashkent.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:56.650
At the end of nineteen thirty-four,
Niyazov graduated as a civil engineer.

00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:01.449
The family came home so poor
that his wife's one dress was patched over

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:05.290
and over, and he borrowed two from his
sister before they visited relatives.

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:08.350
Then he went to Chirchik, near Tashkent,

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:12.629
where a chain of hydroelectric stations
and a great chemical works were rising

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:16.129
on the river, to make nitrogen
fertiliser for the cotton fields.

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:18.309
Over six years he rose

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:22.410
from senior foreman to deputy head of a
works administration on the construction.

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:34.319
In nineteen forty he moved
into the party apparatus,

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.240
as head of the Central Committee's
construction department.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.839
In January nineteen forty-one he became

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.180
the republic's
People's Commissar of Finance.

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:47.300
Five months later,
Germany invaded the Soviet Union.

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:53.400
Within a year, Uzbekistan had taken
in industry on a scale it had never known.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.200
Between one hundred and seventy and one
hundred and ninety evacuated plants,

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:01.259
by different counts,
were restarted in the republic.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:04.359
An aircraft factory evacuated from near

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:09.160
Moscow was building transport planes
in Tashkent by January nineteen forty-two.

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:11.720
Moscow paid for the factories.

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:15.120
The republic's own budget,
which Niyazov ran,

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:18.880
carried what had to stretch
to meet the newcomers: schools,

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:21.999
hospitals, housing and local services.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:27.180
In April nineteen forty-two, on the first
morning of a new state war loan,

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:32.000
the republic's subscribers pledged some
eighty-four million rubles before noon.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:34.920
People came, too.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:40.539
Officials today count more than one and a
half million evacuees in Uzbekistan during

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:44.779
the war, among them more than two hundred
thousand children without parents.

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:48.439
By February nineteen forty-two, Tashkent

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:52.779
alone had registered about one hundred
and fifty-six thousand Jewish refugees,

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:58.140
who had fled the German advance from
Ukraine, Belarus, the Baltic and Poland.

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:01.640
For many of them,
Tashkent was the city of bread.

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:05.260
Uzbek families took orphaned
children into their homes.

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:07.879
In twenty twenty-two, a monument

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:11.360
was unveiled in Tashkent to thank
the people who sheltered them.

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:15.019
Others came under guard,

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:19.080
as Koreans from the Soviet Far East
had come in nineteen thirty-seven.

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:22.920
In May nineteen forty-four,
on Stalin's orders,

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:26.479
the whole Crimean Tatar people
was deported from Crimea,

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:30.580
and about a hundred and fifty thousand
of them were sent to Uzbekistan.

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:34.460
Thousands died on the way,
and in the first year of exile.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:40.140
That November, about fifty-two thousand
Meskhetian Turks followed, from Georgia.

00:07:46.667 --> 00:07:49.426
In September nineteen forty-six he became

00:07:49.427 --> 00:07:52.107
Deputy Chairman of the republic's
Council of Ministers.

00:07:52.927 --> 00:07:56.247
On the seventeenth of March nineteen
forty-seven, at forty-three,

00:07:57.027 --> 00:08:00.147
he was elected Chairman
of the Presidium of the Supreme Soviet.

00:08:00.847 --> 00:08:05.387
In protocol he was now head of state:
he signed the republic's decrees,

00:08:05.907 --> 00:08:09.506
appointed its ministers between sessions,
and conferred its honours.

00:08:09.507 --> 00:08:13.167
Real power,
as everywhere in the Soviet Union,

00:08:13.527 --> 00:08:18.387
lay with the party, and its First
Secretary in Tashkent was Usman Yusupov.

00:08:19.697 --> 00:08:21.407
These were years of water.

00:08:22.247 --> 00:08:27.087
On the Syr Darya, the Farkhad dam, begun
in wartime as a people's construction,

00:08:27.547 --> 00:08:30.726
formed its reservoir
in nineteen forty-seven and sent

00:08:30.727 --> 00:08:35.646
its first power the next February, opening
the way for water into the Hungry Steppe.

00:08:35.647 --> 00:08:40.087
In nineteen fifty, the first stage
of the Kattakurgan reservoir,

00:08:40.507 --> 00:08:43.006
the Uzbek Sea, was finished, holding

00:08:43.007 --> 00:08:47.427
some seven hundred million cubic metres
for the fields of the Zarafshan valley.

00:08:57.617 --> 00:09:01.946
On the fifth of April nineteen fifty,
Moscow called Yusupov away,

00:09:01.947 --> 00:09:05.787
to run a new Ministry of Cotton Growing
for the whole Soviet Union.

00:09:06.387 --> 00:09:09.406
That same month,
the Central Committee in Tashkent

00:09:09.407 --> 00:09:14.647
elected Aminjon Niyazov First Secretary
of the Communist Party of Uzbekistan.

00:09:15.347 --> 00:09:17.926
Since nineteen forty-seven
he had been Chairman

00:09:17.927 --> 00:09:22.667
of the Presidium of the Supreme Soviet
of the Uzbek Soviet Socialist Republic.

00:09:23.427 --> 00:09:26.027
Now, in his new role as First Secretary,

00:09:26.687 --> 00:09:29.987
he began to look for a candidate
he could endorse to succeed him.

00:09:31.547 --> 00:09:36.126
How the choice was made is a story
told in his family by his son Adkham,

00:09:36.127 --> 00:09:38.447
and remembered by colleagues
who were there.

00:09:39.267 --> 00:09:42.987
The first candidate was the President
of the Uzbek Academy of Sciences,

00:09:43.627 --> 00:09:46.467
the mathematician Tashmukhamed Sarymsakov.

00:09:47.287 --> 00:09:51.407
But Sarymsakov was a true scholar,
with no wish for high office,

00:09:51.867 --> 00:09:56.307
and he asked the President of the Soviet
Academy of Sciences, Sergei Vavilov,

00:09:56.687 --> 00:09:58.627
to plead with Stalin to release him.

00:09:59.287 --> 00:10:04.667
Stalin agreed, and telephoned Niyazov
himself: he was to propose someone else.

00:10:05.327 --> 00:10:08.187
The session of the Supreme Soviet
had already been called,

00:10:08.467 --> 00:10:10.007
and there was no other candidate.

00:10:11.427 --> 00:10:15.786
Not long before, the young chairman
of the Uzbek Writers' Union had given

00:10:15.787 --> 00:10:19.467
his report at the union's meeting,
and it had made a strong impression.

00:10:20.367 --> 00:10:24.147
Niyazov had been in the hall,
with his friend General Ivan Petrov,

00:10:24.567 --> 00:10:26.867
commander
of the Turkestan Military District.

00:10:27.467 --> 00:10:32.007
He remembered that speech, talked it over,
and put the young man's name forward.

00:10:32.887 --> 00:10:37.586
On the twenty-first of August nineteen
fifty, the Supreme Soviet released Niyazov

00:10:37.587 --> 00:10:42.587
from the post and elected Sharaf Rashidov
Chairman of the Presidium in his place.

00:10:43.467 --> 00:10:49.026
So, by chance, by a personal impression,
and against the clock, began the public

00:10:49.027 --> 00:10:53.157
career of a man who would lead Uzbekistan
for nearly a quarter of a century.

00:10:54.797 --> 00:10:56.497
Niyazov now led the republic.

00:10:57.197 --> 00:10:59.947
Under him, from April nineteen fifty-one,

00:10:59.948 --> 00:11:05.017
Nuritdin Mukhitdinov chaired the Council
of Ministers, and Rashidov the Presidium.

00:11:06.397 --> 00:11:10.256
The republic's first business was cotton,
and Moscow set the plan.

00:11:11.514 --> 00:11:13.093
The record holds other work, too:

00:11:13.094 --> 00:11:17.714
belts of vegetable and potato farms
around Tashkent and the other cities,

00:11:18.414 --> 00:11:22.734
a meat and dairy base around the capital,
a power station begun at Angren,

00:11:23.094 --> 00:11:26.474
and a medical institute founded
at Andijan in nineteen fifty-five.

00:11:29.142 --> 00:11:32.342
Uranium mined in Uzbekistan
helped supply the programme

00:11:32.382 --> 00:11:35.782
that produced the first
Soviet atomic bombs.

00:11:35.822 --> 00:11:38.842
Mining began here in nineteen forty-six.

00:11:38.882 --> 00:11:44.162
Because uranium was being mined in Uzbekistan,
Amin Irmatovich Niyazov,

00:11:44.202 --> 00:11:47.262
while leading the republic,
pressed the Soviet leadership

00:11:47.302 --> 00:11:51.302
to organize nuclear research
in Uzbekistan itself.

00:11:51.342 --> 00:11:56.122
In nineteen fifty-six, Igor Kurchatov
visited Tashkent and Bukhara.

00:11:56.162 --> 00:12:00.122
Amin Irmatovich Niyazov
helped initiate the creation

00:12:00.162 --> 00:12:02.762
of the Institute of Nuclear Physics.

00:12:02.802 --> 00:12:08.122
Founded in July nineteen fifty-six, after he had
left the post of First Secretary,

00:12:08.162 --> 00:12:12.362
it became an enduring investment
in the republic’s scientific future.

00:12:13.001 --> 00:12:15.831
Niyazov also made a major contribution

00:12:15.832 --> 00:12:19.551
to building the Tashkent–Kokand
road through Kamchik Pass.

00:12:20.269 --> 00:12:24.409
This route across the mountains linked
the Fergana Valley directly to the rest

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:29.164
of Uzbekistan, providing a more practical
connection for people and goods.

00:12:30.634 --> 00:12:33.494
After Stalin's death in March
nineteen fifty-three,

00:12:33.534 --> 00:12:36.314
Moscow began to court
the new nations of Asia,

00:12:36.354 --> 00:12:38.634
and Tashkent became its front door.

00:12:38.998 --> 00:12:42.025
On the fourteenth of June
nineteen fifty-five,

00:12:42.026 --> 00:12:45.218
Niyazov, as First Secretary
and leader of the republic,

00:12:45.778 --> 00:12:49.297
stood on the Tashkent airfield
with Mukhitdinov and Rashidov,

00:12:49.298 --> 00:12:51.378
to greet the Prime Minister of India,

00:12:51.858 --> 00:12:55.018
Jawaharlal Nehru,
and his daughter, Indira Gandhi.

00:12:55.818 --> 00:12:59.518
The official welcome speech
and reception fell to Mukhitdinov,

00:12:59.558 --> 00:13:01.897
who chaired the Council
of Ministers under him;

00:13:01.898 --> 00:13:05.208
the dinner in their honour
was given by Niyazov himself.

00:13:06.468 --> 00:13:09.088
Four months later,
the Prime Minister of Burma,

00:13:09.488 --> 00:13:12.408
U Nu, entered the Soviet Union
through Tashkent,

00:13:12.608 --> 00:13:15.048
and Niyazov was
in the welcoming line again.

00:13:16.408 --> 00:13:20.688
That autumn, Khrushchev and Bulganin
set out on a long tour of India,

00:13:21.048 --> 00:13:22.288
Burma and Afghanistan.

00:13:23.028 --> 00:13:27.348
On the seventeenth of November they passed
through Tashkent on their way south.

00:13:28.408 --> 00:13:29.907
In Washington, the CIA

00:13:29.908 --> 00:13:33.068
briefed the National Security Council
on their itinerary.

00:13:43.198 --> 00:13:44.708
They came back the same way.

00:13:45.348 --> 00:13:48.648
At ten past two on the afternoon
of the nineteenth of December,

00:13:49.228 --> 00:13:53.268
their plane landed in Tashkent,
and Niyazov led the welcoming party.

00:13:54.048 --> 00:13:55.808
That evening, the paper reported,

00:13:56.348 --> 00:13:59.848
more than three hundred thousand
people filled the city's Red Square.

00:14:01.168 --> 00:14:03.108
The next day, at the Navoi Theatre,

00:14:03.688 --> 00:14:06.727
Khrushchev addressed a conference
of the republic's cotton growers.

00:14:06.728 --> 00:14:10.268
The main report was given
not by the First Secretary,

00:14:10.908 --> 00:14:15.608
but by his subordinate, the Chairman
of the Council of Ministers, Mukhitdinov.

00:14:15.963 --> 00:14:17.163
Niyazov spoke too.

00:14:17.774 --> 00:14:21.952
At eight in the morning on the
twenty-first, in bad weather, he stood

00:14:21.953 --> 00:14:25.933
at the head of the line on the airfield
as the two leaders left for Moscow.

00:14:27.893 --> 00:14:30.973
The next day, the twenty-second
of December nineteen fifty-five,

00:14:32.113 --> 00:14:34.793
the Central Committee met
in regular plenum and,

00:14:35.033 --> 00:14:38.953
in the words of its notice,
considered an organisational question.

00:14:39.893 --> 00:14:43.632
It released Aminjon Niyazov
from his duties as First Secretary,

00:14:43.633 --> 00:14:46.833
and elected Nuritdin Mukhitdinov
in his place.

00:14:47.853 --> 00:14:51.033
The notice, printed the following morning,
gave no reason.

00:14:51.973 --> 00:14:54.332
On the same front page, the daily cotton

00:14:54.333 --> 00:14:58.773
return showed five of the republic's
ten regions still short of their plan.

00:15:00.306 --> 00:15:02.766
In Washington, the Evening Star took note.

00:15:03.486 --> 00:15:05.986
"It is safe to say," its editorial began,

00:15:06.626 --> 00:15:11.306
"that very few Americans sat up and took
notice when a gentleman named Amin I.

00:15:11.406 --> 00:15:15.386
Niyazov was fired as First Secretary
of the Uzbek Communist Party.

00:15:16.046 --> 00:15:19.746
Yet this little-noticed event may not
have been devoid of significance."

00:15:20.626 --> 00:15:24.666
Khrushchev, the paper observed,
had been "outspokenly critical,

00:15:24.966 --> 00:15:28.165
as is his way,
of the Uzbek government and its party

00:15:28.166 --> 00:15:30.866
leaders for failing
to increase cotton production."

00:15:32.284 --> 00:15:35.084
According to his family, the principal
reason for

00:15:35.124 --> 00:15:39.204
Niyazov’s removal was his disagreement
with Soviet leaders over

00:15:39.244 --> 00:15:43.364
expanding cotton production. He opposed
plans that relied

00:15:43.404 --> 00:15:46.984
on excessive irrigation and diverting the
rivers feeding the

00:15:47.024 --> 00:15:51.944
Aral Sea, fearing environmental
consequences for future generations.

00:15:52.759 --> 00:15:54.839
He argued for growing the crop steadily,

00:15:55.299 --> 00:15:58.039
with careful management
of the water the republic had,

00:15:58.439 --> 00:16:02.499
and he would not drive his people and
their rivers at the pace Moscow demanded.

00:16:04.309 --> 00:16:05.659
But the drive came.

00:16:06.079 --> 00:16:10.038
In August nineteen fifty-six,
eight months after his fall,

00:16:10.039 --> 00:16:13.958
a decree adopted on the proposal
of the new Uzbek leadership

00:16:13.959 --> 00:16:18.618
ordered three hundred thousand hectares
of the Hungry Steppe brought under cotton.

00:16:18.619 --> 00:16:20.738
In the decades that followed,

00:16:20.739 --> 00:16:24.178
more and more of the two rivers
was drawn off for the fields,

00:16:24.179 --> 00:16:29.818
and every autumn schoolchildren, students
and office workers were sent out to pick.

00:16:29.819 --> 00:16:32.359
The Aral Sea began to die.

00:16:32.999 --> 00:16:37.599
By twenty eighteen it had shrunk to about
a seventh of its size in nineteen sixty.

00:16:38.459 --> 00:16:42.499
Those decisions were made after his fall,
by the men who followed him.

00:16:52.629 --> 00:16:54.649
For three months he had no post.

00:16:55.509 --> 00:16:58.948
On the nineteenth of March
nineteen fifty-six he was appointed

00:16:58.949 --> 00:17:01.449
the republic's Minister
of Municipal Economy.

00:17:02.329 --> 00:17:03.969
Years before, in Moscow,

00:17:04.609 --> 00:17:09.269
his old friend Urumbai Ashurov, who would
go on to lead the party in Tajikistan,

00:17:09.749 --> 00:17:11.809
had kept urging him to come to Dushanbe:

00:17:12.469 --> 00:17:15.828
we will make you Minister
of Municipal Economy.

00:17:15.829 --> 00:17:19.069
Niyazov always refused until
he had finished the Academy.

00:17:19.929 --> 00:17:22.709
Now, given that very post, he smiled.

00:17:23.609 --> 00:17:28.069
It seems, he said in Uzbek,
that this post was written on my forehead.

00:17:29.479 --> 00:17:32.728
From nineteen fifty-seven
to nineteen sixty he headed

00:17:32.729 --> 00:17:36.369
the republic's main administration
for gas and municipal services.

00:17:37.259 --> 00:17:39.739
In that post, he helped lay the foundations

00:17:39.779 --> 00:17:43.739
for bringing natural gas
to homes and rural communities,

00:17:43.779 --> 00:17:48.099
work that supported its wider expansion
in the decades that followed.

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:54.300
In his later years and until his final
days, Niyazov remained active in public
life,

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:59.340
taking part in community events and
advising behind the scenes whenever needed

00:17:59.380 --> 00:18:03.860
on strategic questions affecting
Uzbekistan’s well-being.

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:09.860
Leaders and communities respected his
tireless service and modest way of life.

00:18:12.709 --> 00:18:15.669
On the sixth of November
nineteen seventy-three,

00:18:15.709 --> 00:18:21.309
for his seventieth birthday, he was named
Honoured Builder of the Uzbek Republic,

00:18:21.349 --> 00:18:25.089
for his personal contribution
to capital construction.

00:18:25.129 --> 00:18:30.709
Seven weeks later, on the twenty-sixth
of December, he died in Tashkent.

00:18:30.749 --> 00:18:33.469
Thousands filed past his coffin.

00:18:33.509 --> 00:18:38.289
He received a state funeral and was
buried in the Chigatai cemetery.

00:18:38.329 --> 00:18:43.909
His obituary was signed by Sharaf Rashidov
and the whole leadership of the republic.

00:18:44.867 --> 00:18:48.067
His life spanned decades of change
in Uzbekistan,

00:18:48.107 --> 00:18:51.607
from the Fergana Valley to construction,

00:18:51.647 --> 00:18:55.727
wartime finance, and the leadership
of the republic.

00:18:56.327 --> 00:18:58.707
In his later years, he continued his

00:18:58.747 --> 00:19:01.967
public work in municipal
and gas services.

00:19:02.507 --> 00:19:05.567
His story remains part of the history
of his country

00:19:05.607 --> 00:19:07.927
and the memories of his family.
